.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cc
- the Whitesmiths C compiler supervisor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cc [ option ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cc drives the original Whitesmiths toolchain: it preprocesses with
cpp, parses with cp1, generates code with cp2, assembles with as and
links with link, so one command turns source into a running program.
A file ending in .c is compiled, one in .s is assembled, and .o and
.a files are handed to the linker.  With no -c or -S the result is an
executable called a.out, or the name given with -o.
.sp
The passes are looked up in /bin, so cc runs beside them rather than
carrying its own.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-o name	Name the output file rather than a.out.

.ti -10
-c	Compile and assemble, but do not link; leave the .o.

.ti -10
-S	Compile but do not assemble; leave the .s.

.ti -10
-E	Preprocess only; leave the .i.

.ti -10
-O	Run the Whitesmiths optimizer between code generation and
assembly.

.ti -10
-p	Keep the intermediate files the passes produce.

.ti -10
-n	Print the commands without running them.

.ti -10
-v	List each command as it is run.

.ti -10
-x opt	Pass opt on to cp2, the code generator.

.ti -10
-i dir	Add dir to the preprocessor's include search path.

.ti -10
-I dir	Add dir to the include path, in -i form.

.ti -10
-l lib	Link with liblib.a.  The default link is libS.a, libmd.a,
libu.a and libm.a, so -lS need not be given for stdio.

.ti -10
-s	Strip symbols from the output.

.ti -10
-P prefix	Prefix the tool names with prefix instead of /bin/.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
